+### Float atomic adds through a compare-and-swap loop
+
+- **Symptom:** a compute dispatch of one or a few thread groups with trivial maths takes hundreds of microseconds (Metal System Trace); the time is the same at any resolution.
+- **Cause:** the translator writes NVN's float atomic add (RED / ATOM .F32.FTZ.RN) as a compare-and-swap loop on the word (`InstEmitMemory.cs` `EmitAtomicAddF32`). When every thread of the dispatch adds into the same few words (a reduction), the threads serialise on one cache line and retry under contention: 256 threads into one word are 256 round trips to the atomic unit.
+- **Fix:** `red_sum` (`dm/gfx/tools/dmsh_redsum.py`, a step of `dmsh_build.py` for compute shaders, before `first_store` / `tg_spill`), on with the profile key `shader_redsum` (`DMSH_REDSUM=1` / `0` overrides it for one build): a loop none of whose values is used after it becomes `simd_sum` of the value and one add by the SIMD group's first active lane, Metal's native `atomic_fetch_add_explicit` on an `atomic_float` in device memory (threadgroup memory keeps a compare-and-swap, made by that lane). A uniform index (literals and constant-buffer words at literal offsets) takes one sum; any other index groups the lanes by word, one word a round (`simd_min` over pending indices, explicit zero masks and a uniform `simd_any` loop). Every active lane executes each reduction, avoiding if-conversion mixing different addresses. The sum's rounding order differs from the serial one, as the atomics' order already does run to run.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md): 6 compute shaders, 23 loops (the exposure and cubemap-to-SH reductions). On an M5, 1bba09ec4bc8ff67 361 → 9 us, 6e28359aa3558f61 289 → 70 us, 77c4bfc0d562c310 88 → 9 us: 0.69 ms of the frame.
+- **Check:** `python3 dm/gfx/tests/test_dmsh_redsum.py`.
